{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0729"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge science/chemistry","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/science__chemistry.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",1074],["+","Bronze's golden-looking cousin is brass. Its two main components are copper and zinc: at least half copper, usually about 60 to 70 per cent, with the rest mostly zinc. The Romans struck coins from it, and because it is easy to work, resists corrosion and has a warm gold colour, it is the metal of door handles, ships' fittings, cartridge cases and the whole \"brass section\" of the orchestra.\n\n"],["=",89],["+","\n| Brass | Copper and zinc |"]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",1032],["+","Der goldglänzende Verwandte der Bronze ist das Messing. Seine beiden Hauptbestandteile sind Kupfer und Zink: mindestens zur Hälfte Kupfer, meist etwa 60 bis 70 Prozent, der Rest überwiegend Zink. Die Römer prägten Münzen daraus, und weil es sich leicht bearbeiten lässt, korrosionsbeständig ist und eine warme Goldfarbe hat, ist es das Metall von Türklinken, Schiffsbeschlägen, Patronenhülsen und der Blechbläser im Orchester.\n\n"],["=",87],["+","\n| Messing | Kupfer und Zink |"]]}]},
